package netparam

// The editable fields of NetworkParam row 0, grouped by the view that shows
// them. Offsets and the hidden 0x7C field come from NetworkParam.xml read in
// order; descriptions are the editor's own, from community findings.
var Fields = []Field{
	{
		Key: "maxBreakInTargetListCount", View: Invader, Label: "Target list size",
		Offset: 0x70, Kind: S32, Min: 1, Max: 20, Default: 5,
		Short:   "Maximum candidates returned per server query. The client works through the list attempting connections before restarting the interval and requesting a new one.\nRaise → more connection attempts per cycle; fewer wasted intervals when hosts are unavailable.\nLower → smaller batch; the interval restarts more often even when valid targets exist.",
		Long:    "When you use an invasion item — the Bloody Finger, Recusant Finger, or similar — your game does not immediately connect you to someone else's world. It first contacts FromSoftware's matchmaking servers and asks for a list of players it could potentially invade. Those players are online, in an area that allows invasions, and fall within the character level range the game requires for a match.\n\nThis parameter controls how many names are on that list. Your game then works through the list one by one, trying to establish a connection with each player. If a connection succeeds, you invade. If it fails — because that player just died, already has an invader, moved to a safe area, or has connection problems — the game crosses off that name and tries the next one.\n\nIf every name on the list fails, the game waits the Search Interval and then asks the server for a completely fresh list. Raising this number means you get more attempts per round before that waiting period kicks in, which directly reduces search time in busy sessions.\n\nLowering it has the opposite effect: a shorter list is exhausted quickly, forcing the game back to the waiting cycle more often even when plenty of valid targets exist on the server.",
		Details: "PARAMDEF SortID: 200 — 乱入先取得数 (NET_BREAKIN_PARAM)\nMemory offset: 0x70  |  Type: int32 (LittleEndian.Uint32)\nVanilla value: 5\nValidation: 1–20\nNo cross-field dependencies.",
	},
	{
		Key: "breakInRequestAreaCount", View: Invader, Label: "Area search count",
		Offset: 0x7C, Kind: S32, Min: 1, Max: 20, Default: 5,
		Confidence: CommunityInferred,
		Warning:    "This field is deliberately hidden by FromSoftware in the public PARAMDEF (labelled as unused padding). Its server-side effect is community-inferred. Treat changes conservatively.",
		Short:      "Number of geographic zones the server queries when building the candidate list. A hidden field — effect is community-inferred, not officially documented.\nRaise → broader map coverage; more candidates in low-density or late-game regions.\nLower → narrower search scope; list may thin out in end-game or off-peak areas.",
		Long:       "Elden Ring's world is divided into geographic regions — Limgrave, Liurnia of the Lakes, Caelid, the Altus Plateau, and so on. When the server builds your invasion candidate list, it does not necessarily search the entire game world at once. This parameter influences how many of those regions it looks across.\n\nIn early, heavily populated areas like Limgrave, this setting matters little — there are always enough players nearby regardless of how many zones are searched. But in end-game regions like Farum Azula or Miquella's Haligtree, the active player population is much smaller, and a narrow geographic search may return very few or no results at all.\n\nRaising this value tells the server to cast a wider net, which helps keep the candidate list populated during off-peak hours or in less-active late-game areas.\n\nAn important note: FromSoftware deliberately obscured this field in their official parameter files, labelling it as empty unused space. Its exact server-side behaviour has been worked out by the community through testing rather than from any official documentation. Changes are generally safe but should be treated with more caution than fully confirmed parameters.",
		Details:    "PARAMDEF SortID: 203 — labelled 'dummy8 pad[4]' in the public PARAMDEF to conceal it from standard editors (NET_BREAKIN_PARAM)\nMemory offset: 0x7C  |  Type: int32 (read as uint32, treated as signed)\nVanilla value: 5\nValidation: 1–20\nExact server-side behavior is community-inferred, not officially documented.",
	},
	{
		Key: "breakInRequestIntervalTimeSec", View: Invader, Label: "Search interval",
		Offset: 0x74, Kind: F32, Min: 4, Max: 30, Default: 30,
		Short:   "Cooldown between successive candidate list requests. The client waits this long after exhausting a list — or between requests when no targets connect — before querying the server again.\nRaise → longer gaps between polls; recently eligible hosts may be missed.\nLower → more frequent queries; newly eligible hosts appear in the next batch sooner.",
		Long:    "Your game does not search for invasion targets in a continuous stream. It works in cycles: request a list of candidates from the server, try each one, then wait a set amount of time before requesting a new list. This parameter is the length of that wait.\n\nAt the game's default of 30 seconds, up to half a minute can pass between rounds of searching. During that gap, any player who just summoned a cooperator and became invasible will not appear in your results until the next cycle starts. This is why invasions often feel slow even when the server has plenty of eligible targets.\n\nReducing this to 6–10 seconds means your game checks in with the server much more frequently. A player who just became invasible 5 seconds ago will likely appear in your very next request rather than making you wait through the rest of a 30-second gap.\n\nGoing below about 4 seconds offers diminishing returns — the server itself needs time to process each request, so querying faster than the round-trip allows does not meaningfully improve results. Values in the 6–10 second range are the practical sweet spot.",
		Details: "PARAMDEF SortID: 201 — 乱入リクエスト間隔[秒] (NET_BREAKIN_PARAM)\nMemory offset: 0x74  |  Type: float32\nVanilla value: 30.0 s\nValidation: 4–30 s\nThe server also has a minimum dispatch rate independent of this setting.",
	},
	{
		Key: "breakInRequestTimeOutSec", View: Invader, Label: "Request timeout",
		Offset: 0x78, Kind: F32, Min: 4, Max: 20, Default: 20,
		Warning: "Lowering below 7–8 s starts filtering valid cross-region connections — those players can legitimately take 8–20 s to establish. Same-region connections typically complete in 2–5 s; setting the timeout near the same-region floor costs you the cross-region pool without a meaningful speed gain.",
		Short:   "Per-candidate handshake deadline. After selecting a host from the list, this is how long the client waits for the session to establish before abandoning that entry and moving to the next.\nRaise → slow or cross-regional connections get more time to complete; recommended floor is 7–8 s.\nLower → dead sessions cleared faster, but values below 7 s start cutting valid cross-region matches before they finish.",
		Long:    "Once the game picks a specific player from its candidate list to invade, it starts a connection process with that player's game. Both sides need to acknowledge each other, agree on the session parameters, and establish a direct link. This typically takes a few seconds when everything is working normally.\n\nSometimes that process stalls entirely. The target player may have lost their internet connection, their game may have crashed, they may have moved to an area where invasions are disabled, or their network setup may be blocking the connection. When any of those things happen, the connection attempt just hangs.\n\nThis parameter is how long the game waits in that stalled state before concluding the connection is not going to work and moving on to the next name on the list.\n\nRaising it gives slow or geographically distant connections more time to succeed — useful if you frequently try to invade players in regions far from your own, where the connection setup naturally takes longer. Lowering it means you stop waiting on clearly failing connections sooner and move to the next candidate faster, though legitimate connections that are just a bit slow may get cut before they finish.",
		Details: "PARAMDEF SortID: 202 — 乱入リクエストタイムアウト時間[秒] (NET_BREAKIN_PARAM)\nMemory offset: 0x78  |  Type: float32\nVanilla value: 20.0 s\nValidation: 4–20 s",
	},
	{
		Key: "reloadSignIntervalTime2", View: FindSigns, Label: "Sign refresh interval",
		Offset: 0x1C, Kind: F32, Min: 10, Max: 60, Default: 60,
		Short:   "Full cache invalidation cadence during active play. At each interval the client discards its local sign list entirely and downloads a fresh copy from the server.\nRaise → signs grow stale; new placements appear late, expired ones linger.\nLower → sign pool stays current; recent placements and removals are reflected faster.",
		Long:    "Summon signs — the golden and red marks you see on the ground — are not streamed to your game in real time. Your game periodically downloads a snapshot of available signs from the server, stores that snapshot locally, and displays those stored signs on the ground. Between downloads, the signs you see are whatever was in the last snapshot, even if the actual situation has changed.\n\nThis parameter controls how often that snapshot is completely replaced during normal play — while you are walking through an area, standing near signs, or approaching a boss fog gate. At the game's default of 60 seconds, a phantom who placed a sign immediately after your last download will not appear for you until the next one — potentially nearly a full minute later. At the same time, a sign from a phantom who was already summoned or disconnected may remain visible on your screen until that refresh clears it.\n\nReducing this value makes those refreshes happen more often, keeping the signs you see much closer to the server's current state. At 10–15 seconds, new signs show up shortly after being placed and expired ones disappear promptly, which makes the co-op experience feel significantly more alive and responsive.\n\nRaising it beyond 60 seconds is workable in quiet areas with little sign activity, but creates a noticeably stale and confusing experience in busy co-op zones.",
		Details: "PARAMDEF SortID: 103 — 召喚サイン再取得待機時間[秒] (NET_SUMMON_SIGN_PARAM)\nMemory offset: 0x1C  |  Type: float32\nVanilla value: 60 s\nValidation: 10–60 s\nThis is the 'active' refresh timer. The companion sparse-area timer (SortID 102, Sparse-area Refresh Interval) is at offset 0x18 and is an Advanced parameter.",
	},
	{
		Key: "reloadSignTotalCount", View: FindSigns, Label: "Sign pool (total)",
		Offset: 0x20, Kind: S32, Min: 5, Max: 64, Default: 20,
		Warning: "Must satisfy the invariant: Sign Pool (Per Cell) ≤ Sign Pool (Total) ≤ Sign Buffer Cap. Violating it produces inconsistent pool behaviour.",
		Short:   "Client-side cache capacity after each refresh — the maximum number of signs stored and displayed simultaneously. Does not affect how many the server holds, only how many the client stores.\nRaise → more signs visible at once; essential in high-density areas where 20 hides most available phantoms.\nLower → smaller local pool; limits visible signs regardless of how many the server has.",
		Long:    "When your game downloads the sign snapshot from the server, it does not show you every sign the server knows about. It only stores and displays up to this many signs at a time.\n\nAt the default of 20, you see at most 20 signs on the ground simultaneously. In popular co-op areas — just before a major boss fog gate, for example — the server may have 50, 100, or more active signs from players waiting to be summoned. Your game shows you only 20 of them, hiding the rest entirely.\n\nRaising this value simply increases how many signs your game holds in its local cache after each refresh. You are not making additional server requests — you are asking for a bigger slice of the available signs each time. The improvement is most noticeable in high-activity areas where the default of 20 creates a misleadingly thin-looking sign pool.\n\nThis value must be at least as large as Sign Pool (Per Cell), and must not exceed Sign Buffer Cap. See the Warning above.",
		Details: "PARAMDEF SortID: 105 — 召喚サイン所持可能数上限(全体) (NET_SUMMON_SIGN_PARAM)\nMemory offset: 0x20  |  Type: int32 (PARAMDEF notes 'actually u8' for value range; stored as 32-bit)\nVanilla value: 20\nValidation: 5–64\nCross-field constraints: must be ≥ ReloadSignCellCount (0x24) and ≤ SingGetMax (0x60). Breaking these invariants produces inconsistent pool behavior.",
	},
	{
		Key: "reloadSignCellCount", View: FindSigns, Label: "Sign pool (per cell)",
		Offset: 0x24, Kind: S32, Min: 1, Max: 32, Default: 10,
		Warning: "Must not exceed Sign Pool (Total). ValidateNetworkParams enforces this constraint.",
		Short:   "Per-zone contribution cap within the total pool. Prevents a single dense cell from monopolising the entire cache by limiting how many signs the client accepts from any one spatial zone.\nRaise → dense zones can supply a larger share of the pool; reduces geographic diversity.\nLower → caps each zone's contribution; forces broader geographic spread across the pool.",
		Long:    "The game world is divided into an invisible spatial grid of zones for multiplayer matchmaking. When your game collects signs to fill its local pool, it gathers them from all the zones around your current position. Without any restriction, one extremely popular zone — the approach to a famous boss, for instance — could fill your entire sign pool by itself, leaving nothing from any surrounding area.\n\nThis parameter prevents that by setting a maximum number of signs that any single zone can contribute to your pool. Even if a zone has a hundred active signs, your client will only take this many from it before moving on to the next zone.\n\nThe practical effect is geographic variety in your sign pool: results come from multiple areas rather than being dominated by one hotspot. This matters most in areas where multiple zones of varying population density sit close together.\n\nThis value must always be lower than or equal to Sign Pool (Total). Setting it higher than the total pool is meaningless — the total cap is the absolute ceiling on how many signs you can hold, regardless of how many any single zone could supply.",
		Details: "PARAMDEF SortID: 106 — 召喚サイン所持可能数上限(セル) (NET_SUMMON_SIGN_PARAM)\nMemory offset: 0x24  |  Type: int32 (PARAMDEF notes 'actually u8' for value range; stored as 32-bit)\nVanilla value: 10\nValidation: 1–32\nCross-field constraint: must be ≤ ReloadSignTotalCount (0x20). ValidateNetworkParams enforces this.",
	},
	{
		Key: "singGetMax", View: FindSigns, Label: "Sign buffer cap",
		Offset: 0x60, Kind: S32, Min: 10, Max: 64, Default: 32,
		Warning: "Must be ≥ Sign Pool (Total). Also, the game server may enforce its own independent cap — client values above that server limit are silently ignored.",
		Short:   "Maximum signs the server returns in a single response. Acts as a hard ceiling on Sign Pool (Total) — if smaller than the total pool, the pool can never be fully filled regardless of activity.\nRaise → server can return larger batches; required before increasing the total pool beyond the current cap.\nLower → server responses truncated; Sign Pool (Total) is effectively capped at this value even if set higher.",
		Long:    "When your game asks the server for signs, the server decides how many to include in its response. This parameter tells the server the maximum number you are willing to receive. No matter how large your local sign pool is set, you cannot receive more signs per refresh than this number allows.\n\nThis creates a dependency: Sign Pool (Total) is how many signs your game stores locally, but Sign Buffer Cap is the most you can ever receive in one server response to fill that pool. If you raise Sign Pool (Total) to 64 but leave this at the default of 32, the server will still only ever send 32 signs — leaving your 64-slot pool permanently half empty.\n\nThe correct order when increasing sign visibility is: raise this cap first, then raise the total pool, then adjust the per-cell cap proportionally.\n\nBe aware that the game's own servers may also enforce an independent ceiling that no client-side setting can change. If you set this very high and see no improvement in the number of signs visible, you may have reached that server-enforced limit.",
		Details: "PARAMDEF SortID: 122 — サイン取得上限 (NET_SUMMON_SIGN_PARAM)\nMemory offset: 0x60  |  Type: int32\nVanilla value: 32\nValidation: 10–64\nCross-field constraint: must be ≥ ReloadSignTotalCount (0x20). ValidateNetworkParams enforces this.\nNote: the game server may impose its own independent cap. Client-side values above that server cap are silently clamped server-side.",
	},
	{
		Key: "signDownloadSpan", View: FindSigns, Label: "Sign download span",
		Offset: 0x64, Kind: F32, Min: 10, Max: 30, Default: 30,
		Short:   "Trickle update interval — minimum gap between incremental background downloads that propagate individual sign changes between full cache refreshes.\nRaise → sign changes accumulate until the next full refresh; pool feels more static between cycles.\nLower → individual changes propagate faster; smoother, more continuous updates between full refreshes.",
		Long:    "The game uses two separate mechanisms to keep your sign pool up to date. The first is the full refresh controlled by Sign Refresh Interval: every so often, your game completely replaces its local sign list with a fresh download from the server.\n\nThe second mechanism — the one this parameter controls — is a lighter background update that runs between those full refreshes. Rather than waiting for the next complete reset, your game periodically fetches just the changes: signs that newly appeared and signs that were removed, without rebuilding the entire list from scratch.\n\nThink of the full refresh as replacing a newspaper with a new edition, and the download span as getting individual article corrections between editions.\n\nLowering this value makes those between-refresh corrections arrive more frequently, so the sign pool feels more continuously alive rather than staying static for long periods and then jumping all at once during a full refresh. Raising it makes updates more intermittent — you see the pool remain unchanged for longer and then suddenly shift when the next full refresh lands.",
		Details: "PARAMDEF SortID: 123 — サインダウンロードスパン (NET_SUMMON_SIGN_PARAM)\nMemory offset: 0x64  |  Type: float32\nVanilla value: 30 s\nValidation: 10–30 s\nWorks in conjunction with ReloadSignIntervalTime2 (full refresh, 0x1C) and SignUpdateSpan (upload direction, 0x68).",
	},
	{
		Key: "summonTimeoutTime", View: FindSigns, Label: "Summon timeout",
		Offset: 0x8, Kind: F32, Min: 10, Max: 120, Default: 45,
		Advanced: true,
		Warning:  "This is a handshake floor, not a speed lever — lowering it does not make summons faster, only cuts connections that are still establishing. Cross-region co-op can take 15–30 s; setting this below ~20 s risks filtering valid summons. The refresh parameters in this view are the effective speed controls.",
		Short:    "Global handshake deadline for co-op summons. Times out the full phantom-loading process from sign interaction to in-world appearance. On expiry the sign silently reappears; both players see a failed loading screen.\nRaise → marginal long-distance connections get more time; stuck loading screens run longer on failure.\nLower → failed summons clear faster, but values below ~20 s start cutting cross-region connections that were still establishing.",
		Long:     "When a host interacts with your summon sign, both your game and theirs begin a connection process. Your character needs to be loaded into their world, your equipment synchronised, and a stable link established between the two games. This normally takes between 5 and 20 seconds when everything works correctly.\n\nSometimes the process stalls. Poor network conditions, firewall settings blocking the direct connection, or the host moving to a different area can all prevent it from completing. When that happens, both players see a spinning loading indicator that simply stays there — you cannot interact or do anything. This parameter defines the maximum time that loading process is allowed to run before the game abandons it.\n\nWhen this timer expires, your sign silently reappears on the ground and both players return to their game with no explanation of what happened.\n\nThe vanilla value is 45 seconds, which is already generous — most successful summons complete well under 20 seconds. Lowering it means failed attempts resolve faster, reducing time spent watching a stuck loading screen. The trade-off is that slow but potentially successful connections — most common when matching with players in distant countries — may be cut before they finish.\n\nNote: this setting is shared across all summoning types in the game, not just signs placed with the Furlcalling Finger Remedy.",
		Details:  "PARAMDEF SortID: 2 — 召喚希望タイムアウト時間[秒] (NET_COMMON_PARAM — shared across all summoning types, not sign-specific)\nMemory offset: 0x08  |  Type: float32\nVanilla value: 45 s (confirmed from actual PS4 save file)\nValidation: 10–120 s",
	},
	{
		Key: "reloadSignIntervalTime1", View: FindSigns, Label: "Sparse-area refresh interval",
		Offset: 0x18, Kind: F32, Min: 0, Max: 120, Default: 0,
		Advanced: true, Confidence: CommunityInferred,
		Short:   "Separate refresh rate for areas with low sign density — when few signs or players are present nearby. At 0 (shipped default) the system is disabled and the standard refresh interval applies everywhere. Non-zero enables a two-speed mode.\nSet to 0 → single rate in all contexts; vanilla behaviour.\nSet above 0 → slower polling in sparse sign areas; reduces traffic without slowing the experience in busy areas.",
		Long:    "Normally, the Sign Refresh Interval you set applies all the time — whether you are standing at a fog gate surrounded by signs, or in a remote area where few players have placed any.\n\nThis parameter can optionally create a second, slower refresh rate that applies when the area has low sign density — when few signs are present nearby and frequent polling would return the same empty results.\n\nThe game ships this field at 0, which means the system is disabled: the active rate from Sign Refresh Interval applies everywhere regardless of local sign density. This is the simplest and most predictable behaviour, and for most players it is the right choice.\n\nSetting a non-zero value here enables the two-speed system. For example: Sign Refresh Interval at 10 seconds for busy sign areas, this field at 60 seconds for sparse ones. The game switches between the two rates based on local sign density, reducing server traffic in quiet areas without making the sign pool feel slow near active fog gates.\n\nThe exact threshold the game uses to classify an area as sparse versus active is community-inferred — the precise switching condition is not fully documented.",
		Details: "PARAMDEF SortID: 102 — 召喚サイン再取得待機時間(過疎時)[秒] (NET_SUMMON_SIGN_PARAM)\nMemory offset: 0x18  |  Type: float32\nActual shipped regulation value: 0 (PARAMDEF lists default = 1 but the regulation ships 0)\nValidation: 0–120 s  |  0 is explicitly valid — means 'use active timer (reloadSignIntervalTime2)'\nCompanion to ReloadSignIntervalTime2 (active timer, offset 0x1C).",
	},
	{
		Key: "signCellRangeH", View: FindSigns, Label: "Cell range (horizontal)",
		Offset: 0x3C, Kind: S32, Min: 1, Max: 5, Default: 1,
		Advanced: true, Confidence: CommunityInferred,
		Warning: "Raising cell range without proportionally increasing Sign Pool (Total) and Sign Buffer Cap causes extra results to be silently truncated.",
		Short:   "Horizontal radius of the spatial search volume in matchmaking cells. Value of 1 = current cell only; each increment expands outward by one ring, multiplying the searched area.\nRaise → exponentially more cells searched; more signs from a wider horizontal area.\nLower → narrower horizontal search; players spread across adjacent cells may be missed.",
		Long:    "The game divides the world into a three-dimensional grid of invisible zones called cells for multiplayer matchmaking. When your game fetches summon signs, it searches for signs within a volume of cells centred on your position.\n\nThis parameter controls how far that search extends horizontally. At the default value of 1, the search covers only the single cell you are standing in. Raising it to 2 expands the search to include one ring of surrounding cells — a 3×3 grid of 9 cells. Raising it to 3 covers a 5×5 grid of 25 cells. Each step outward multiplies the searched area significantly.\n\nThe horizontal range matters most in wide-open areas like Limgrave, Liurnia, or the Mountaintops of the Giants, where players naturally spread out across many cells. In tight dungeon corridors the benefit is smaller since adjacent cells contain fewer players anyway.\n\nIf you raise this value without also raising Sign Pool (Total) and Sign Buffer Cap, the additional cells are searched but the extra signs they contain are simply cut off to fit the existing pool size. You must increase the cache capacity alongside the search volume for the change to have any visible effect on how many signs you see.",
		Details: "PARAMDEF SortID: 113 — 召喚サイン取得セル範囲(水平) (NET_SUMMON_SIGN_PARAM)\nMemory offset: 0x3C  |  Type: int32 (stored as u32 — 4 bytes despite PARAMDEF note 'actually u8' for value range)\nVanilla value: 1\nValidation: 1–5\nLayout verification: 3 u32 fields × 4 B (0x3C/0x40/0x44) + 6 f32 keyword-scale fields × 4 B (0x48–0x5F) = 36 B → SingGetMax at 0x60 ✓",
	},
	{
		Key: "signCellRangeUp", View: FindSigns, Label: "Cell range (upward)",
		Offset: 0x40, Kind: S32, Min: 1, Max: 5, Default: 1,
		Advanced: true, Confidence: CommunityInferred,
		Warning: "Raising cell range without proportionally increasing Sign Pool (Total) and Sign Buffer Cap causes extra results to be silently truncated.",
		Short:   "Upward component of the 3D search volume — how many cell layers above the player are included when fetching signs.\nRaise → signs from elevated positions and upper floors become visible.\nLower → only the current vertical cell is searched; players on higher floors are excluded.",
		Long:    "Elden Ring has significant vertical complexity — many dungeons, towers, and buildings have multiple floors, and the underground regions of the world sit directly below the surface. The matchmaking cell grid extends vertically as well as horizontally.\n\nThis parameter controls how many cell layers above your current position are searched when fetching signs. At the default of 1, only your current vertical level is covered. A host standing at the base of Raya Lucaria Academy cannot see signs from a phantom standing on the upper floors, because they occupy different vertical cells.\n\nRaising this value bridges those vertical gaps. Setting it to 2 means your search reaches one cell layer upward, connecting floors that would otherwise be separated in the sign pool.\n\nThis is most impactful in: multi-story legacy dungeons (Stormveil, Raya Lucaria, Elphael Brace of the Haligtree), areas with significant elevation variation across a short horizontal distance, and transition zones between the surface world and underground areas.\n\nAs with horizontal range, raising this without also increasing Sign Pool (Total) and Sign Buffer Cap produces no visible improvement — additional cells are searched but results are silently truncated to fit the existing cache.",
		Details: "PARAMDEF SortID: 114 — 召喚サイン取得セル範囲(上方向) (NET_SUMMON_SIGN_PARAM)\nMemory offset: 0x40  |  Type: int32 (stored as u32 — same 4-byte storage note as SignCellRangeH)\nVanilla value: 1\nValidation: 1–5\nWorks with SignCellRangeH (0x3C) and SignCellRangeDown (0x44) to define the full 3D search volume.",
	},
	{
		Key: "signCellRangeDown", View: FindSigns, Label: "Cell range (downward)",
		Offset: 0x44, Kind: S32, Min: 1, Max: 5, Default: 1,
		Advanced: true, Confidence: CommunityInferred,
		Warning: "Raising cell range without proportionally increasing Sign Pool (Total) and Sign Buffer Cap causes extra results to be silently truncated.",
		Short:   "Downward component of the 3D search volume — how many cell layers below the player are included when fetching signs.\nRaise → signs from lower floors and underground areas become visible to players above.\nLower → only the current vertical cell is searched; players in underground areas beneath you are excluded.",
		Long:    "The downward counterpart to Cell Range (Upward). While that parameter lets you see signs from players above you, this one opens visibility to players below — and symmetrically lets hosts above see signs from you when you are in a lower area.\n\nElden Ring connects its surface and underground worlds through vertical layers: parts of Limgrave sit above Siofra River, areas of Leyndell connect to the underground city, and cave systems run beneath outdoor zones. A host standing on the surface normally cannot see signs from phantoms exploring directly below them in an underground region, because they occupy different vertical cells.\n\nRaising the downward range opens up those cross-level connections. A surface host with a downward range of 2 can see signs from phantoms one vertical cell below — potentially in the underground region directly beneath the surface area.\n\nFor most situations, setting upward and downward range to matching values makes sense, since you generally want symmetric vertical coverage. If your session is entirely underground or entirely on the surface, you can tilt the balance toward the relevant direction.\n\nAs with all cell range parameters, the pool size must scale proportionally — raise Sign Pool (Total) and Sign Buffer Cap alongside range to avoid silent result truncation.",
		Details: "PARAMDEF SortID: 115 — 召喚サイン取得セル範囲(下方向) (NET_SUMMON_SIGN_PARAM)\nMemory offset: 0x44  |  Type: int32 (stored as u32 — same 4-byte storage note as SignCellRangeH)\nVanilla value: 1\nValidation: 1–5\nAll three cell-range parameters (0x3C/0x40/0x44) define a rectangular 3D search volume. Adjust together when increasing overall spatial coverage.",
	},
	{
		Key: "updateSignIntervalTime", View: PlaceSign, Label: "Sign upload interval",
		Offset: 0x28, Kind: F32, Min: 10, Max: 30, Default: 30,
		Short:   "Re-registration heartbeat for your own placed sign — how often the client confirms sign validity to the server to prevent silent expiry.\nRaise → less frequent heartbeats; sign risks expiring silently during extended waits.\nLower → more frequent confirmation pings; substantially reduces the chance of your sign disappearing from others' pools.",
		Long:    "When you place a summon sign, you are telling the server that you want to be summoned from that location. But signs on the server expire after a period of inactivity — if the server does not hear from your game confirming that you are still there and still waiting, it eventually removes your sign from the lists it provides to other players.\n\nThis parameter controls how often your game automatically sends that confirmation — a regular message that keeps your sign marked as active. Think of it as a heartbeat for your placed sign.\n\nAt the default of 30 seconds, your game confirms twice per minute. This is sufficient for short waits. But if you sit near a fog gate for ten or fifteen minutes waiting to be summoned, there is a meaningful chance that a network hiccup causes one of those confirmation messages to fail to reach the server, and your sign silently disappears from everyone else's view with no notification to you.\n\nReducing this to 10–15 seconds means your game sends confirmations more frequently, making it much harder for a single dropped message to cause your sign to expire. Raising it beyond 30 seconds saves a small amount of server traffic but meaningfully increases the risk of silent sign loss during long waits.",
		Details: "PARAMDEF SortID: 107 — 召喚サイン更新待機時間[秒] (NET_SUMMON_SIGN_PARAM)\nMemory offset: 0x28  |  Type: float32\nVanilla value: 30 s\nValidation: 10–30 s\nThis governs re-registration of the player's own sign specifically. Distinct from SignDownloadSpan (0x64) and SignUpdateSpan (0x68), which handle bulk background data synchronization.",
	},
	{
		Key: "signUpdateSpan", View: PlaceSign, Label: "Sign upload span",
		Offset: 0x68, Kind: F32, Min: 10, Max: 60, Default: 60,
		Short:   "Outgoing sync interval — minimum gap between background uploads of local sign state to the server. Upload counterpart to Sign Download Span.\nRaise → your client reports local sign activity less often; the server's view of your area grows stale.\nLower → more frequent outgoing updates; the server stays better informed, improving sign accuracy for nearby players.",
		Long:    "The sign sync system works in two directions. The previous parameters cover how your game downloads sign information from the server. This one covers the other direction: how often your game uploads information about your local sign activity back to the server.\n\nThis upload matters because the server's database needs to stay accurate. If signs are being placed, removed, or interacted with in your area, the server needs to know about it to give accurate information to other players who are searching nearby.\n\nThe practical effect of this parameter is mostly indirect — it does not change what you see on screen, but it affects what other players see when they search for signs in your area. Lowering it means your game reports outgoing activity more often, keeping the server's picture of your surroundings more current, which improves the sign experience for nearby players.\n\nRaising it significantly can create a visible lag between local events and when other players see those events reflected in their own sign pools.",
		Details: "PARAMDEF SortID: 124 — サインアップロードスパン (NET_SUMMON_SIGN_PARAM)\nMemory offset: 0x68  |  Type: float32\nVanilla value: 60 s\nValidation: 10–60 s\nUpload counterpart to SignDownloadSpan (0x64). Distinct from UpdateSignIntervalTime (0x28), which handles re-registration of the player's own placed sign specifically.",
	},
	{
		Key: "reloadVisitListCoolTime", View: Hunter, Label: "Visit list cooldown",
		Offset: 0x180, Kind: F32, Min: 5, Max: 20, Default: 20,
		Short:   "Polling interval for the Blue Cipher Ring search loop — how long the client waits between cycles checking the server for an available invaded world.\nRaise → less frequent checks; the game responds to new invasions more slowly.\nLower → more frequent queries; dispatch happens closer to the moment an invasion becomes available.",
		Long:    "When you equip the Blue Cipher Ring, your game runs a background process that repeatedly checks a server for invaded worlds that need a Hunter. It does not watch continuously — it checks, waits a set amount of time, then checks again in an ongoing loop.\n\nThis parameter controls how long that wait is between checks.\n\nAt the default of 20 seconds, your game asks the server roughly three times per minute whether an invaded world is available for you. If an invasion begins in between two of those checks, your game will not see it until the next check fires. In active PvP periods where invasions start and end quickly, missing that window means you never get dispatched for that particular event at all.\n\nReducing this to 5–8 seconds means your game checks much more frequently, dramatically narrowing the gap between when an invasion becomes available and when you are dispatched to it. The practical benefit diminishes below about 3 seconds because the server has its own minimum processing time for each request regardless of how often you ask.\n\nRaising it beyond the default makes you respond to invasions more slowly with no benefit in return.",
		Details: "PARAMDEF SortID: 600 — 指輪検索間隔[秒] (NET_VISIT_PARAM)\nMemory offset: 0x180  |  Type: float32\nVanilla value: 20 s\nValidation: 5–20 s\nGoverns search loop for all Blue Cipher Ring variants. Distinct from Co-op Search Min/Max (0x190/0x194), which govern a separate randomized interval mechanism.",
	},
	{
		Key: "maxVisitListCount", View: Hunter, Label: "Visit list size",
		Offset: 0x18C, Kind: S32, Min: 1, Max: 20, Default: 5,
		Short:   "Candidates per Hunter search cycle — direct analog to Target List Size for invaders. The client tries each entry in sequence; a larger list reduces wasted cycles when many worlds exist but are incompatible.\nRaise → more worlds to attempt per cycle; fewer completely wasted cooldowns.\nLower → smaller batch; the cycle wastes more often on incompatible or unreachable entries.",
		Long:    "Each time the Blue Cipher Ring search cycle fires, your game receives a list of invaded worlds it could potentially enter as a Hunter. It then tries each one in sequence — checking that your character's level is compatible, that there is room for another phantom, and that a connection can be established.\n\nThis parameter controls how long that list is. At the default of 5, your game receives five options per cycle. If all five fail — the invader was already defeated, the host moved to a safe area, level ranges do not match, or connections could not be established — the cycle ends without dispatching you anywhere, and the cooldown begins again.\n\nRaising this value gives more options before a cycle is completely wasted. In active PvP sessions with many simultaneous invasions, the server may have many invaded worlds to offer, but a significant portion may not be compatible with your specific character's level or weapon upgrade. A longer list gives you more chances of finding a compatible match within each cycle rather than waiting through another full cooldown.\n\nThis parameter works exactly the same way as Target List Size in the invasion system — both control how many candidates the game works through per search round.",
		Details: "PARAMDEF SortID: 603 — 指輪検索先取得数 (NET_VISIT_PARAM)\nMemory offset: 0x18C  |  Type: int32 (stored as u32)\nVanilla value: 5\nValidation: 1–20\nDirect analog to MaxBreakInTargetListCount (0x70) for the Hunter role.",
	},
	{
		Key: "maxCoopBlueSummonCount", View: Hunter, Label: "Co-op blue summon cap",
		Offset: 0x184, Kind: S32, Min: 1, Max: 2, Default: 2,
		Warning: "The game server enforces its own session cap that overrides this value in standard online play. Raising above 2 has no observable effect.",
		Short:   "Client-side ceiling on simultaneous Blue Phantoms. Has no observable effect in standard online play — the game server enforces its own cap that overrides this value.\nRaise → no effect in standard play; server cap still limits actual joins.\nLower → artificially restricts Blue Phantom presence below the server's intended limit.",
		Long:    "In an online session, there is a limit to how many Blue Phantoms — Hunters summoned through the Blue Cipher Ring — can be present in a world at the same time. This limit is checked in two places: by your game client locally, and by FromSoftware's servers centrally.\n\nThis parameter controls only the client-side check — what your own game treats as the maximum. The server independently enforces its own separate cap, and that server cap is the one that actually determines how many Blue Phantoms can appear in practice during standard online play.\n\nRaising this above 2 simply removes the client's local check. Since the server still enforces its own limit, the result in normal online play is identical — you cannot increase the actual number of Blue Phantoms in a session by changing this value alone. Doing so would require also changing what the server enforces, which is not possible through save file editing.\n\nLowering it below 2 artificially restricts the game below the server's intended behaviour and reduces the effectiveness of co-op without any benefit. The only situation where this setting has any visible effect is in custom server environments that have independently raised their own session limits.",
		Details: "PARAMDEF SortID: 601 — 救援青霊出現数上限 (NET_VISIT_PARAM)\nMemory offset: 0x184  |  Type: int32 (PARAMDEF notes 'actually u8' for value range; stored as 32-bit)\nVanilla value: 2\nValidation: 1–2\nThe server enforces a separate, independent cap. Client values above that server cap are silently no-ops in standard online play.",
	},
	{
		Key: "reloadSearchCoopBlueMin", View: Hunter, Label: "Co-op search min",
		Offset: 0x190, Kind: F32, Min: 5, Max: 30, Default: 30,
		Warning: "Must not exceed Co-op Search Max. If min > max the random interval selection is undefined.",
		Short:   "Lower bound of the randomised inter-cycle wait. Actual wait is drawn uniformly from [min, max] each cycle; since average = (min + max) ÷ 2, reducing only the minimum has limited impact — reducing the maximum is far more effective.\nRaise → extends even the shortest possible waits.\nLower → reduces the floor; modest gain unless the maximum is also reduced.",
		Long:    "Between each Hunter search cycle, your game waits a random amount of time before checking for available invaded worlds again. That wait is not a fixed duration — it is a random value chosen somewhere between a minimum and a maximum. This parameter sets the minimum.\n\nThe randomness exists for a practical reason: if every player with the Blue Cipher Ring equipped checked the server at exactly the same instant, the resulting traffic spike could cause performance problems for everyone. The random spread distributes server load by having different players check at slightly different times.\n\nThe minimum determines the shortest wait that can ever occur. However, because the actual wait is chosen randomly anywhere between the minimum and the maximum, the average wait is simply halfway between them. At the default values — 30 seconds minimum, 180 seconds maximum — the average wait between cycles is 105 seconds.\n\nReducing only the minimum from 30 to 5 seconds moves the average to about 92 seconds. That is an improvement, but a modest one. The maximum has a far larger effect on the average, which is why reducing both values together produces the most noticeable improvement in how quickly you get dispatched.",
		Details: "PARAMDEF SortID: 604 — 救援青霊リロード時間 最小[sec] (NET_VISIT_PARAM)\nMemory offset: 0x190  |  Type: float32\nVanilla value: 30 s\nValidation: 5–30 s\nCross-field constraint: must be ≤ ReloadSearchCoopBlueMax (0x194). The actual per-cycle wait is drawn from Uniform(min, max). ValidateNetworkParams enforces the min ≤ max invariant.",
	},
	{
		Key: "reloadSearchCoopBlueMax", View: Hunter, Label: "Co-op search max",
		Offset: 0x194, Kind: F32, Min: 20, Max: 180, Default: 180,
		Warning: "Must not be less than Co-op Search Min. ValidateNetworkParams enforces this constraint.",
		Short:   "Upper bound of the randomised inter-cycle wait — the single most impactful parameter for Hunter dispatch frequency. Since average = (min + max) ÷ 2, halving the maximum roughly halves the average wait.\nRaise → longer worst-case waits; Hunter dispatch becomes passive and unpredictable.\nLower → most effective single lever for faster dispatch; eliminates long-wait outliers.",
		Long:    "This is the companion to Co-op Search Min: it sets the upper end of the random wait between Hunter search cycles. The game picks a random duration somewhere between the minimum and this maximum before each new cycle begins.\n\nBecause the wait is uniformly random between the two values, the average comes out to exactly halfway: (minimum + maximum) ÷ 2. The maximum therefore has the greatest influence on how quickly you get dispatched on average.\n\nAt the game's default values — 30 seconds minimum, 180 seconds maximum — the average wait between cycles is 105 seconds. The worst-case wait is 180 seconds, meaning you could sit with the Blue Cipher Ring equipped for three minutes without being dispatched, simply due to bad random luck. Reducing the maximum to 45 seconds while leaving the minimum at 30 drops the average to about 37 seconds.\n\nFor the fastest dispatch, reduce both values together to a narrow range. A minimum of 5 seconds and a maximum of 20 seconds gives an average wait of 12 seconds — approximately 8 times faster than the default — while keeping the randomisation that prevents every Hunter from hitting the server at the same moment.\n\nThis must always be set at or above the minimum. If the maximum falls below the minimum, the random selection becomes undefined.",
		Details: "PARAMDEF SortID: 605 — 救援青霊リロード時間 最大[sec] (NET_VISIT_PARAM)\nMemory offset: 0x194  |  Type: float32\nVanilla value: 180 s\nValidation: 20–180 s\nCross-field constraint: must be ≥ ReloadSearchCoopBlueMin (0x190). ValidateNetworkParams enforces this invariant.",
	},
	{
		Key: "allAreaSearchRateCoopBlue", View: Hunter, Label: "Co-op all-area rate (%)",
		Offset: 0x1D8, Kind: U8, Min: 0, Max: 100, Default: 30,
		Confidence: CommunityInferred,
		Short:      "Probability (0–100) that a given search cycle queries the entire map rather than only the player's local region. Controls geographic scope for the co-op assist trigger.\nRaise → more searches go global; better dispatch rate when local invasion activity is sparse.\nLower → local-focused searches; lower connection latency but fewer dispatches when your area is quiet.",
		Long:       "When the game searches for an invaded world to send you to, it can either search close to where your character currently is on the map, or it can search the entire world regardless of location. Local searches tend to connect you with players who have better network proximity to you, since players in the same region typically share internet infrastructure. However, if no invasions are happening locally, a local-only search will consistently come up empty.\n\nThis parameter controls how often a search covers the whole map instead of staying local. Each cycle the game effectively rolls a random number: if the result falls below this percentage, the search goes global; otherwise it stays local.\n\nAt the default of 30%, roughly one in three searches covers the entire world. During quiet periods when few invasions are happening in your local region, those two out of three local searches will find nothing. Raising this value toward 60–80% means most of your searches cover the full map, dramatically improving dispatch rates when nearby activity is low.\n\nThe trade-off: a global match may connect you with a player in a distant country, which can mean noticeably higher latency. Whether that trade-off is worth it depends on whether you prioritise being dispatched frequently or prioritise connection quality when you are dispatched.",
		Details:    "PARAMDEF SortID: 730 — 全域検索率：救援青霊 (NET_EXTRA_PARAM)\nMemory offset: 0x1D8  |  Type: u8 (1 byte), stored as int32 in the struct\nVanilla value: 30\nValidation: 0–100 (strict percentage)\nThe three all-area rate bytes are stored consecutively: 0x1D8 (CoopBlue), 0x1D9 (VsBlue), 0x1DA (BellGuard).",
	},
	{
		Key: "allAreaSearchRateVsBlue", View: Hunter, Label: "Hunter all-area rate (%)",
		Offset: 0x1D9, Kind: U8, Min: 0, Max: 100, Default: 30,
		Confidence: CommunityInferred,
		Short:      "All-area rate for the 'retribution' Blue Cipher Ring trigger — a second activation pathway distinct from the co-op assist search. Identical mechanic to Co-op All-Area Rate but for a separate trigger type.\nRaise → more global searches for this trigger type.\nLower → geographically constrained searches for this trigger.",
		Long:       "The Blue Cipher Ring can be triggered through more than one pathway. The main one — governed by Co-op All-Area Rate — activates when a player using the White Cipher Ring calls for help. This parameter covers a second trigger type, labelled 'retribution' in the game's internal data.\n\nIn Dark Souls 3, this distinction was clearly visible to players: the Blue Sentinel covenant responded specifically to active invasion events, while the Way of Blue responded to deliberate co-op calls. These were genuinely separate trigger pathways with different search priorities. Elden Ring's data preserves that same internal distinction.\n\nIn Elden Ring's player-facing design, the separation is less explicit. The game does not explain to players exactly when the 'retribution' trigger fires versus the co-op assist trigger, and community testing on the practical difference between the two in Elden Ring has not produced definitive results.\n\nFor practical purposes, keeping this value in line with Co-op All-Area Rate is a reasonable default. Setting them to different values is possible if you want to tune the two trigger types separately, but the exact behavioural difference is not fully established.",
		Details:    "PARAMDEF SortID: 730 — 全域検索率：報復青霊 (NET_EXTRA_PARAM)\nMemory offset: 0x1D9  |  Type: u8 (1 byte), stored as int32 in struct\nVanilla value: 30\nValidation: 0–100\nThe '報復青霊' (retribution/revenge blue spirit) designation likely originates from DS3's Blue Sentinels covenant. In ER the trigger conditions may differ from earlier titles.",
	},
	{
		Key: "allAreaSearchRateBellGuard", View: Hunter, Label: "Bell-guard all-area rate (%)",
		Offset: 0x1DA, Kind: U8, Min: 0, Max: 100, Default: 30,
		Advanced: true, Confidence: Unconfirmed,
		Warning: "In-game trigger conditions in Elden Ring are unconfirmed. This field is a legacy remnant from Dark Souls 2's Bell Keeper covenant and may be vestigial.",
		Short:   "All-area rate for the 'bell guardian' phantom type — a third trigger variant from DS2's Bell Keeper system. No confirmed player-facing trigger for this type exists in Elden Ring; the field may be vestigial.\nRaise → increased global search rate if this trigger is active in ER.\nLower → geographically constrained search; equally uncertain in practice.",
		Long:    "In Dark Souls 2, certain areas of the game world contained bell towers that players could choose to trespass in. A PvP covenant called the Bell Keepers automatically summoned its members to defend those towers against intruders — an early automated, location-based invasion system tied to specific points on the map rather than general PvP activity flags.\n\nThis parameter is a direct descendant of that system. The game's internal data includes a 'bell guardian' phantom type stored immediately after the co-op and retribution all-area rates. Its name in Japanese — 鐘守灰霊 — translates to 'bell guardian spirit' and is the same internal designation used in Dark Souls 2.\n\nElden Ring does not appear to have a bell guardian system in its player-facing content. There are no bell towers with designated defenders, no Bell Keeper-equivalent covenant, and no item described as triggering this phantom type. The parameter exists in the regulation data and holds a non-zero live value, but whether it does anything in Elden Ring is not confirmed by community testing.\n\nThis parameter is exposed here for completeness. Adjusting it alongside the other all-area rates is technically harmless, but any practical effect it may have in Elden Ring is unknown.",
		Details: "PARAMDEF SortID: 730 — 全域検索率：鐘守灰霊 (NET_EXTRA_PARAM)\nMemory offset: 0x1DA  |  Type: u8 (1 byte), stored as int32 in struct\nVanilla value: 30\nValidation: 0–100\nThe '鐘守灰霊' (bell guardian spirit) name connects to DS2's Bell Keeper covenant.\nIn-game trigger conditions in ER: unconfirmed by community testing as of this writing.",
	},
}
